WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.590
&gt;&gt; So, it's my pleasure to
introduce the Yejin Choi,

00:00:07.591 --> 00:00:09.270
who's an associate professor at

00:00:09.271 --> 00:00:10.800
the University of Washington and

00:00:10.801 --> 00:00:13.535
also a Senior Research
Manager at AI, too.

00:00:13.536 --> 00:00:15.000
She's done a number of

00:00:15.001 --> 00:00:16.890
really interesting research
projects over the years.

00:00:16.891 --> 00:00:19.020
It's always fun to watch
what Yejin has been doing,

00:00:19.021 --> 00:00:21.120
including things about
common sense knowledge or

00:00:21.121 --> 00:00:22.500
Language Generation and more

00:00:22.501 --> 00:00:24.220
recently about AI
for Social Good.

00:00:24.221 --> 00:00:25.830
She has had a number of

00:00:25.831 --> 00:00:28.335
really impressive
accolades including

00:00:28.336 --> 00:00:30.150
like the top ten
to watch in 2015,

00:00:30.151 --> 00:00:34.620
from IEEE AI and recipient
of the Marr prize in

00:00:34.621 --> 00:00:36.870
ICCV and also a

00:00:36.871 --> 00:00:37.980
member of the team that won

00:00:37.981 --> 00:00:39.659
the Alexa prize
challenge in 2017,

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.480
so lots of amazing things
to watch for.

00:00:42.481 --> 00:00:46.290
So, well, that's probably enough.

00:00:46.291 --> 00:00:49.455
&gt;&gt; Okay. All right so,

00:00:49.456 --> 00:00:52.255
speaking of
Alexa prize challenge,

00:00:52.256 --> 00:00:55.950
I will start by sharing
brief experience with this.

00:00:55.951 --> 00:00:59.010
So, the goal of
this challenge was to create

00:00:59.011 --> 00:01:01.140
Conversational AI that can make

00:01:01.141 --> 00:01:04.260
a coherent and engaging
conversation with humans.

00:01:04.261 --> 00:01:08.370
The good news is that
the team that I was part of

00:01:08.371 --> 00:01:10.980
thanks to the amazing
students we won

00:01:10.981 --> 00:01:13.605
the competition and it felt
great to be the winner.

00:01:13.606 --> 00:01:16.635
The bad news is
that Conversational AI

00:01:16.636 --> 00:01:20.185
remains to be more or
less unsolved, so,

00:01:20.186 --> 00:01:23.940
especially to our surprise

00:01:23.941 --> 00:01:26.310
we were not a surprising
factor at all is

00:01:26.311 --> 00:01:29.130
that the winning recipe
was not based

00:01:29.131 --> 00:01:32.440
on neural networks with
brute force more data,

00:01:32.441 --> 00:01:35.920
deeper networks, and spiked away

00:01:35.921 --> 00:01:38.540
the reinforcement learning,
that just didn't work.

00:01:38.541 --> 00:01:41.525
So, now that's curious
why would that be,

00:01:41.526 --> 00:01:45.410
because we thought that
neural networks to do

00:01:45.411 --> 00:01:47.795
amazing things like
superhuman performance

00:01:47.796 --> 00:01:50.120
on object recognition
or image captioning.

00:01:50.121 --> 00:01:52.430
A lot of industry replacing

00:01:52.431 --> 00:01:54.125
their long-standing statistical

00:01:54.126 --> 00:01:55.580
machine translation systems

00:01:55.581 --> 00:01:56.960
with neural network models.

00:01:56.961 --> 00:01:59.030
Then of course
a special recognition has

00:01:59.031 --> 00:02:01.235
been working really well and

00:02:01.236 --> 00:02:03.590
even more recently
human-level performance

00:02:03.591 --> 00:02:05.570
on reading
comprehension, and wow!

00:02:05.571 --> 00:02:08.075
and, all these are based on

00:02:08.076 --> 00:02:09.815
very large amount of

00:02:09.816 --> 00:02:13.220
training data and then
sufficiently deep networks,

00:02:13.221 --> 00:02:16.080
so, this should work.

00:02:16.081 --> 00:02:20.090
However, if we look
at closely there are

00:02:20.091 --> 00:02:22.595
significant performance
gaps across

00:02:22.596 --> 00:02:24.660
different types of tasks.

00:02:24.661 --> 00:02:26.715
So, first of all,

00:02:26.716 --> 00:02:29.000
nobody's really reporting on

00:02:29.001 --> 00:02:31.100
superhuman performance on making

00:02:31.101 --> 00:02:32.870
a conversation or summarizing

00:02:32.871 --> 00:02:35.750
a document or composing an email

00:02:35.751 --> 00:02:39.035
on behalf of me or
identifying fake news

00:02:39.036 --> 00:02:41.090
despite the fact that we have

00:02:41.091 --> 00:02:43.520
a lot of data for
all of these where

00:02:43.521 --> 00:02:45.620
if not me at least companies do

00:02:45.621 --> 00:02:48.020
have a lot of data
for any of this,

00:02:48.021 --> 00:02:50.240
and they do have
a lot of GPUs too.

00:02:50.241 --> 00:02:51.995
So, why is it that we don't

00:02:51.996 --> 00:02:54.790
hear about this from the news?

00:02:54.791 --> 00:03:00.110
Even for those applications
for which neural networks do

00:03:00.111 --> 00:03:05.375
perform at super-human level
for some data sets,

00:03:05.376 --> 00:03:07.095
they usually are not very

00:03:07.096 --> 00:03:09.570
robust if given unfamiliar out of

00:03:09.571 --> 00:03:11.940
the domain or adversarial example

00:03:11.941 --> 00:03:14.835
as Jonathan was talking
about this morning.

00:03:14.836 --> 00:03:17.690
So, what's going on here?

00:03:17.691 --> 00:03:21.900
I'm going to argue that in
fact that's because there

00:03:21.901 --> 00:03:24.810
are very fundamentally
two different types

00:03:24.811 --> 00:03:27.090
of applications out there,

00:03:27.091 --> 00:03:30.790
and we're primarily seeing
the advancements for

00:03:30.791 --> 00:03:32.310
type one type task where

00:03:32.311 --> 00:03:35.180
shallow understanding
can get you quite far.

00:03:35.181 --> 00:03:39.290
So, an example is translating
bananas are green in

00:03:39.291 --> 00:03:40.505
English sentence into

00:03:40.506 --> 00:03:44.185
a Polish sentence banana
[inaudible] For this,

00:03:44.186 --> 00:03:45.830
neuron networks are very good at

00:03:45.831 --> 00:03:48.380
learning word level or
phrase-level pattern

00:03:48.381 --> 00:03:50.285
matching so that it can learn

00:03:50.286 --> 00:03:52.670
to translate from one language to

00:03:52.671 --> 00:03:54.725
the other without
really understand

00:03:54.726 --> 00:03:57.305
much about a particular language.

00:03:57.306 --> 00:04:00.200
Whereas, if I were to
make a conversation

00:04:00.201 --> 00:04:02.990
and tell you bananas are
green you might tell me,

00:04:02.991 --> 00:04:06.690
"No they're not" or you might
tell me "They're not ripe".

00:04:06.691 --> 00:04:08.605
If we try to learn

00:04:08.606 --> 00:04:11.340
the mapping function between

00:04:11.341 --> 00:04:14.250
input output parings this way,

00:04:14.251 --> 00:04:16.440
this is only a very
weak alignment

00:04:16.441 --> 00:04:17.990
between the input and output,

00:04:17.991 --> 00:04:19.740
and in fact we need some sort

00:04:19.741 --> 00:04:21.600
of abstraction cognition we're

00:04:21.601 --> 00:04:25.875
reasoning in order to really
know what to say next.

00:04:25.876 --> 00:04:28.380
So, which in turn requires

00:04:28.381 --> 00:04:31.110
knowledge especially
common sense knowledge,

00:04:31.111 --> 00:04:34.110
and ignoring this factor
all together and try to

00:04:34.111 --> 00:04:35.490
learn this mapping function

00:04:35.491 --> 00:04:37.305
just doesn't make a lot of sense.

00:04:37.306 --> 00:04:40.200
So, to make another point

00:04:40.201 --> 00:04:42.840
about how important it
is to be able to read

00:04:42.841 --> 00:04:45.410
between the lines and let's

00:04:45.411 --> 00:04:48.200
think about news headline
cheeseburger stabbing,

00:04:48.201 --> 00:04:51.170
if you give this to state
of the art parser today,

00:04:51.171 --> 00:04:52.340
it's going to tell you that

00:04:52.341 --> 00:04:54.605
cheeseburger is a noun modifying.

00:04:54.606 --> 00:04:57.710
Another word is stabbing
which may be a noun or verb

00:04:57.711 --> 00:05:00.960
depending on which parser
you use, but doesn't matter.

00:05:00.961 --> 00:05:03.680
The fact that one word modifying
the other doesn't tell

00:05:03.681 --> 00:05:06.770
us whether it means someone
stepped a cheeseburger,

00:05:06.771 --> 00:05:08.975
or a cheeseburger step
to someone,

00:05:08.976 --> 00:05:10.400
or a cheeseburger stepped on

00:05:10.401 --> 00:05:13.310
another cheeseburger
and so forth.

00:05:13.311 --> 00:05:16.565
You know what may have
been the actual meaning

00:05:16.566 --> 00:05:19.820
of this news headline
because you're

00:05:19.821 --> 00:05:23.090
able to fill in these missing
information from

00:05:23.091 --> 00:05:24.860
the sentence so that
there are probably

00:05:24.861 --> 00:05:27.255
two people involved in this act,

00:05:27.256 --> 00:05:29.210
even though they were not even

00:05:29.211 --> 00:05:31.955
mentioned in the original
news headline.

00:05:31.956 --> 00:05:33.785
When we do that,

00:05:33.786 --> 00:05:36.800
it's the common sense
knowledge that we rely on,

00:05:36.801 --> 00:05:38.540
especially physical common sense

00:05:38.541 --> 00:05:40.040
is that it's not possible to

00:05:40.041 --> 00:05:42.390
step somebody using
a cheese burger

00:05:42.391 --> 00:05:43.970
because it's too soft,

00:05:43.971 --> 00:05:49.100
and stabbing someone is
not good and it's immoral,

00:05:49.101 --> 00:05:51.560
so it's more likely to be news

00:05:51.561 --> 00:05:55.780
worthy whereas if you step
a cheeseburger who cares.

00:05:55.781 --> 00:05:58.740
So, when we look at

00:05:58.741 --> 00:06:00.390
and think about different types

00:06:00.391 --> 00:06:01.740
of knowledge in fact there

00:06:01.741 --> 00:06:03.525
has been tremendous amount of

00:06:03.526 --> 00:06:06.420
research before
learning and extracting

00:06:06.421 --> 00:06:08.685
encyclopedic knowledge
like who is

00:06:08.686 --> 00:06:11.745
the president of which country
and born in what year,

00:06:11.746 --> 00:06:13.380
but not knowing this I can make

00:06:13.381 --> 00:06:15.405
a failure okay conversation.

00:06:15.406 --> 00:06:18.420
However, it's really
common sense knowledge

00:06:18.421 --> 00:06:21.370
that we need in order to
make an okay conversation.

00:06:21.371 --> 00:06:25.200
So, these neither
physics type knowledge

00:06:25.201 --> 00:06:28.385
or social norms they have
not been studied very much,

00:06:28.386 --> 00:06:31.375
and if you look up ACL anthology,

00:06:31.376 --> 00:06:35.765
so this is as we all know
the repository of NLP papers,

00:06:35.766 --> 00:06:39.150
most of the papers that
they're to mention the word

00:06:39.151 --> 00:06:40.575
common sense are either from

00:06:40.576 --> 00:06:42.730
80s who are from
the past few years.

00:06:42.731 --> 00:06:46.310
So, nothing in between
complete void.

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:49.770
I think it's in part
because there was

00:06:49.771 --> 00:06:53.535
a tremendous failure with
a common sense in 80's,

00:06:53.536 --> 00:06:56.390
but recently some saw that people

00:06:56.391 --> 00:06:59.120
start to forgetting about
the path to failure.

00:06:59.121 --> 00:07:03.740
So, there's been a few very
interesting data set one of

00:07:03.741 --> 00:07:05.240
which from Microsoft is

00:07:05.241 --> 00:07:08.075
whether rock story
common sense data set.

00:07:08.076 --> 00:07:10.925
So, for all of these tasks

00:07:10.926 --> 00:07:14.300
basically just
brute force, more data,

00:07:14.301 --> 00:07:17.030
larger networks
don't really go very

00:07:17.031 --> 00:07:20.545
far and we somehow need
different game plan.

00:07:20.546 --> 00:07:22.340
So, to be honest I don't really

00:07:22.341 --> 00:07:24.395
know what they should be but

00:07:24.396 --> 00:07:26.510
for lack of any better
idea I'm going to

00:07:26.511 --> 00:07:29.420
talk about two different
directions, a,

00:07:29.421 --> 00:07:32.060
thinking about
neural network architectures

00:07:32.061 --> 00:07:34.010
so they can better model

00:07:34.011 --> 00:07:36.500
the latent process
in our mind when

00:07:36.501 --> 00:07:40.300
understanding and
reasoning about text,

00:07:40.301 --> 00:07:42.935
abstracting away from
the surface patterns.

00:07:42.936 --> 00:07:46.100
Then, simultaneously
we might want to

00:07:46.101 --> 00:07:49.355
think more about
representation formalisms,

00:07:49.356 --> 00:07:51.470
orthogonal to neural networks,

00:07:51.471 --> 00:07:54.830
and really think about how
can we possibly organize

00:07:54.831 --> 00:07:56.705
and learn common sense knowledge

00:07:56.706 --> 00:07:59.105
using language as scaffoldings.

00:07:59.106 --> 00:08:02.750
Now, usually in this spectrum

00:08:02.751 --> 00:08:05.600
I talk about these three
pieces shown here,

00:08:05.601 --> 00:08:11.370
but the second one will be
described by Antoine today.

00:08:11.371 --> 00:08:15.630
So, I'm going to only
give you a teaser

00:08:15.631 --> 00:08:17.220
for that and instead talk about

00:08:17.221 --> 00:08:19.980
dynamic entities in
neural language models.

00:08:19.981 --> 00:08:22.830
So, let us begin
with the first part.

00:08:22.831 --> 00:08:25.395
So, you know checklist the model,

00:08:25.396 --> 00:08:27.750
was originally
designed to to think

00:08:27.751 --> 00:08:32.250
about particular kind of
language generation challenge.

00:08:32.251 --> 00:08:35.240
In particular, there are

00:08:35.241 --> 00:08:38.280
three different types
of generation setting,

00:08:38.281 --> 00:08:40.485
case one is a small
input small output

00:08:40.486 --> 00:08:42.070
like machine translation,

00:08:42.071 --> 00:08:44.460
case two is a big
input small output

00:08:44.461 --> 00:08:46.305
like document summarization,

00:08:46.306 --> 00:08:48.600
and these are two cases
have been studied

00:08:48.601 --> 00:08:51.025
a lot more in the past.

00:08:51.026 --> 00:08:54.000
Whereas case three is
the case when there may be

00:08:54.001 --> 00:08:56.910
only very sketchy input
small input and then

00:08:56.911 --> 00:08:59.880
suddenly we have to
generate the bigger output.

00:08:59.881 --> 00:09:03.130
For this there are
two unique challenges.

00:09:03.131 --> 00:09:05.515
A, there's an information gap.

00:09:05.516 --> 00:09:09.200
So, the machine has to have
a creative power to fill in

00:09:09.201 --> 00:09:10.730
a lot of information that was not

00:09:10.731 --> 00:09:13.340
even quite in
the input, and then,

00:09:13.341 --> 00:09:15.650
the second challenge
is that the moment you

00:09:15.651 --> 00:09:17.720
start generating
more than one sentence

00:09:17.721 --> 00:09:22.330
it's very easy to make problems,

00:09:22.331 --> 00:09:25.435
so, coherency becomes
a big challenge as well.

00:09:25.436 --> 00:09:28.190
So, a little bit
more formally the

00:09:28.191 --> 00:09:31.055
task that we're going to
think about momentarily,

00:09:31.056 --> 00:09:34.680
is that there's going to be
an input which is a title,

00:09:34.681 --> 00:09:36.620
and an agenda which is a list

00:09:36.621 --> 00:09:39.260
of items that you
want to talk about.

00:09:39.261 --> 00:09:41.650
Then the output
will be a paragraph

00:09:41.651 --> 00:09:44.270
or a document where the multiple

00:09:44.271 --> 00:09:48.460
sentences that tries to
achieve the goal implied by

00:09:48.461 --> 00:09:51.040
the title and then
tries to make use

00:09:51.041 --> 00:09:54.630
of only and all of
the agenda items.

00:09:54.631 --> 00:09:57.425
For now, we're going to
use a recipe generation

00:09:57.426 --> 00:09:59.950
as a running task
running example,

00:09:59.951 --> 00:10:02.220
but later the model
will be applied to

00:10:02.221 --> 00:10:06.815
this dialogue response
to generation as well.

00:10:06.816 --> 00:10:12.170
So, at the beginning we thought
that in order to generate

00:10:12.171 --> 00:10:14.270
recipe wish should
first to parse it

00:10:14.271 --> 00:10:18.520
because that's what a lot
of people do in NLP.

00:10:18.521 --> 00:10:20.005
Once we parse it,

00:10:20.006 --> 00:10:22.030
we are going to abstract away

00:10:22.031 --> 00:10:25.640
some prototypical
graph structure out

00:10:25.641 --> 00:10:28.130
of it from which perhaps we

00:10:28.131 --> 00:10:31.410
can sample a graph which
is like it's explaining,

00:10:31.411 --> 00:10:34.455
and then convert to
that graph down to raw text.

00:10:34.456 --> 00:10:36.830
So, there was our original plan,

00:10:36.831 --> 00:10:40.365
and we even worked on
the first segment and managed to

00:10:40.366 --> 00:10:44.375
write a paper about parsing
recipes into action graphs.

00:10:44.376 --> 00:10:45.825
But then you realize,

00:10:45.826 --> 00:10:48.340
we realized that doing

00:10:48.341 --> 00:10:50.820
this entire so code is quite hard

00:10:50.821 --> 00:10:54.030
because errors are
start to propagating.

00:10:54.031 --> 00:10:56.760
It was really unclear
what to do with this.

00:10:56.761 --> 00:10:58.850
I'm still wanting to do this,

00:10:58.851 --> 00:11:01.695
but in the meanwhile we were

00:11:01.696 --> 00:11:03.320
wondering whether
we could somehow

00:11:03.321 --> 00:11:05.530
simplify the whole process.

00:11:05.531 --> 00:11:07.040
That's when we heard

00:11:07.041 --> 00:11:09.260
about encoder-decoder
architecture in

00:11:09.261 --> 00:11:11.960
neural network literature
which apparently work

00:11:11.961 --> 00:11:15.240
great for a of the task
like machine translation.

00:11:15.241 --> 00:11:18.910
So, let's encode
the recipe title into vector,

00:11:18.911 --> 00:11:20.700
and then decode the entire recipe

00:11:20.701 --> 00:11:22.790
out and see what happens.

00:11:22.791 --> 00:11:24.510
Sausage sandwiches.

00:11:24.511 --> 00:11:35.250
So, this is what happens.

00:11:35.251 --> 00:11:38.500
I clicked on purpose
worse example however,

00:11:38.501 --> 00:11:40.560
recurrent neural networks tend to

00:11:40.561 --> 00:11:43.245
repeat itself a lot like
a sandwich sandwich.

00:11:43.246 --> 00:11:47.090
Eventually, it overcomes
the sandwich overdose

00:11:47.091 --> 00:11:51.240
but still things mostly be
repeated that listed twice.

00:11:51.241 --> 00:11:54.665
So, why does this not work
very well for us when

00:11:54.666 --> 00:11:58.260
it does really work really
well for a lot of other task?

00:11:58.261 --> 00:12:00.935
Well, it doesn't work well when

00:12:00.936 --> 00:12:02.675
there's only a very
weak alignment

00:12:02.676 --> 00:12:03.970
between the input and the output.

00:12:03.971 --> 00:12:06.970
That's what I was trying
to tell you all here about

00:12:06.971 --> 00:12:10.370
different input
output comparisons

00:12:10.371 --> 00:12:12.000
in this particular task

00:12:12.001 --> 00:12:14.050
only six to 10% of words

00:12:14.051 --> 00:12:16.385
in the output came from
the input which means

00:12:16.386 --> 00:12:19.390
90-94 percent of the time

00:12:19.391 --> 00:12:21.465
Apple came out of nothing almost,

00:12:21.466 --> 00:12:23.240
or almost that way for

00:12:23.241 --> 00:12:25.615
the neural networks, or
encoder-decoder architecture.

00:12:25.616 --> 00:12:28.950
So, the rest has to come
from somewhere else,

00:12:28.951 --> 00:12:32.430
and neural network
the off-the-shelf models

00:12:32.431 --> 00:12:33.760
don't operate very well.

00:12:33.761 --> 00:12:35.925
So, with this in mind,

00:12:35.926 --> 00:12:38.310
the high level idea is that is it

00:12:38.311 --> 00:12:41.800
possible to compose a
neural network architecture that

00:12:41.801 --> 00:12:45.600
could have this mental map of

00:12:45.601 --> 00:12:51.125
different items that you might
want to check as you go?

00:12:51.126 --> 00:12:54.310
So, that's what we did with
neural checklist model.

00:12:54.311 --> 00:12:57.375
But since this one is
a little bit hard to stare at,

00:12:57.376 --> 00:13:01.360
let's look at a much
simplified version as follows.

00:13:01.361 --> 00:13:05.340
So, we're going to make
garlic tomato salsa based on

00:13:05.341 --> 00:13:09.800
this visualization where we
encode the title into vector.

00:13:09.801 --> 00:13:13.730
There's a checklist that
has a list of agenda items,

00:13:13.731 --> 00:13:16.630
and then at each time step,

00:13:16.631 --> 00:13:21.810
this is one unit of
some recurrent neural network.

00:13:21.811 --> 00:13:23.385
In our case it's a GRU but

00:13:23.386 --> 00:13:25.335
that particular choice
it doesn't matter.

00:13:25.336 --> 00:13:27.260
So, internally, it's going to

00:13:27.261 --> 00:13:29.970
make this three-way decision,

00:13:29.971 --> 00:13:33.860
whether I am about to
generate a word that is

00:13:33.861 --> 00:13:37.320
non-ingredient word or
new ingredient word

00:13:37.321 --> 00:13:38.875
or none of the above.

00:13:38.876 --> 00:13:41.850
So, let us see how it works.

00:13:41.851 --> 00:13:44.710
So, let's just say so far we
generated the "Chop the".

00:13:44.711 --> 00:13:46.960
Internally, the model is

00:13:46.961 --> 00:13:49.200
going to look at
its own context and

00:13:49.201 --> 00:13:53.470
decided that it's time to
use one of the new items.

00:13:53.471 --> 00:13:58.750
So, it's going to do softmax
over all the new items,

00:13:58.751 --> 00:14:01.980
and then that just say tomatoes
got the highest score.

00:14:01.981 --> 00:14:03.910
So, we check that off from

00:14:03.911 --> 00:14:06.825
the checklist and move forward.

00:14:06.826 --> 00:14:08.660
So, the second sentence

00:14:08.661 --> 00:14:10.685
might start to weigh
the "Dice the".

00:14:10.686 --> 00:14:15.060
At this point the network
might internally decide that,

00:14:15.061 --> 00:14:17.715
oh, let's use
another unused item,

00:14:17.716 --> 00:14:20.580
and softmax or do

00:14:20.581 --> 00:14:25.200
the probability production
only over the unused part,

00:14:25.201 --> 00:14:28.560
and let's say, onions were
the second-best choice,

00:14:28.561 --> 00:14:31.935
and check that off from the
checklist and move forward.

00:14:31.936 --> 00:14:34.220
Now, something interesting
is about to happen.

00:14:34.221 --> 00:14:36.720
So, "Add to" at

00:14:36.721 --> 00:14:38.485
this point the contextually

00:14:38.486 --> 00:14:40.410
it's the moment when you go back

00:14:40.411 --> 00:14:44.020
to one of the already introduced
items that's just how

00:14:44.021 --> 00:14:48.470
the discourse accused
are indicating to us,

00:14:48.471 --> 00:14:50.880
and that's also what
the network picks up as well.

00:14:50.881 --> 00:14:53.075
So, "Add to" what

00:14:53.076 --> 00:14:57.135
should we use between
tomatoes and onions?

00:14:57.136 --> 00:14:58.405
&gt;&gt;Tomatoes.

00:14:58.406 --> 00:15:00.540
&gt;&gt; Tomatoes. Because that's what

00:15:00.541 --> 00:15:02.815
was introduced earlier on,

00:15:02.816 --> 00:15:05.330
and the discourse convention
you said that that's

00:15:05.331 --> 00:15:08.140
what we do mention and
natural glands to do that.

00:15:08.141 --> 00:15:11.405
So, in fact all this checklist

00:15:11.406 --> 00:15:15.800
is probabilistic and
were black and white.

00:15:15.801 --> 00:15:18.120
So, it's based on the

00:15:18.121 --> 00:15:21.765
ten the cumulation
of attention scores,

00:15:21.766 --> 00:15:24.580
which reflects how much the model

00:15:24.581 --> 00:15:28.240
thinks that it made use of
a particular ingredient.

00:15:28.241 --> 00:15:30.940
Then the three-way
classification is

00:15:30.941 --> 00:15:33.790
also soft in the sense
it's an interpolation

00:15:33.791 --> 00:15:35.800
of the three different
language model

00:15:35.801 --> 00:15:37.315
like components where

00:15:37.316 --> 00:15:41.855
the first one is generic
language model like RNNs,

00:15:41.856 --> 00:15:43.440
or GRUs, or LSTMs.

00:15:43.441 --> 00:15:45.310
Whereas the other two are based

00:15:45.311 --> 00:15:47.375
on attention mechanisms looking

00:15:47.376 --> 00:15:48.980
at either used the portion or

00:15:48.981 --> 00:15:51.690
unused portion of
the agenda item.

00:15:51.691 --> 00:15:56.990
So, we did comparisons
automatically,

00:15:56.991 --> 00:15:59.630
and of course the story is

00:15:59.631 --> 00:16:02.470
that we do better
than other baselines.

00:16:03.310 --> 00:16:06.570
So we borrowed machine
translation majors,

00:16:06.571 --> 00:16:08.105
and when you just look at

00:16:08.106 --> 00:16:10.940
this number looks
really very low.

00:16:10.941 --> 00:16:14.660
So, you probably want
to see actual samples.

00:16:14.661 --> 00:16:15.875
So, I'm going to show that.

00:16:15.876 --> 00:16:18.855
But briefly this one
shows what fraction of

00:16:18.856 --> 00:16:22.950
agenda items the model
is making use of,

00:16:22.951 --> 00:16:27.590
and the regular
encoder-decoder architecture

00:16:27.591 --> 00:16:30.285
with or without
attentions practically

00:16:30.286 --> 00:16:34.310
ignores what's given in the
input because they are not

00:16:34.311 --> 00:16:37.070
very when the input
output alignment is

00:16:37.071 --> 00:16:38.490
so weak they're not very good

00:16:38.491 --> 00:16:40.130
at actually learning the mapping.

00:16:40.131 --> 00:16:42.365
So, mostly they're
ignored whereas

00:16:42.366 --> 00:16:45.565
checklist can make use
of much bigger portion.

00:16:45.566 --> 00:16:47.245
So, example.

00:16:47.246 --> 00:16:49.070
Skillet chicken rice.

00:16:49.071 --> 00:16:52.170
Baseline. That's regular RNNs.

00:16:52.171 --> 00:16:54.130
In a large skillet,

00:16:54.131 --> 00:16:55.390
brown chicken in oil.

00:16:55.391 --> 00:16:57.205
Add chicken and rice cook.

00:16:57.206 --> 00:17:00.695
Add more rice and broth them
cook some more and so forth.

00:17:00.696 --> 00:17:03.100
So, stirring more rice,

00:17:03.101 --> 00:17:05.135
stirring more rice, keep cooking.

00:17:05.136 --> 00:17:07.360
And probably it's edible,

00:17:07.361 --> 00:17:09.545
safe to eat in the end,

00:17:09.546 --> 00:17:12.750
but somehow there's not
much of a coherency

00:17:12.751 --> 00:17:16.140
so checklist on the other hand
does much better job.

00:17:16.141 --> 00:17:19.000
So, in a largest skillet heat
rice and onion.

00:17:19.001 --> 00:17:20.450
Add carrots and mushrooms cook.

00:17:20.451 --> 00:17:22.795
Stir constantly until
hot and bubbly.

00:17:22.796 --> 00:17:25.090
Stirring seasonings,
cook some more.

00:17:25.091 --> 00:17:27.030
Stirring chicken and
then cook some more,

00:17:27.031 --> 00:17:28.225
and then serve over rice.

00:17:28.226 --> 00:17:31.380
So, overall the coherency

00:17:31.381 --> 00:17:33.230
suddenly become much better even

00:17:33.231 --> 00:17:35.775
though in this model
we are not even doing

00:17:35.776 --> 00:17:38.820
any fancy modelling of
discourse in any measure.

00:17:38.821 --> 00:17:41.000
But it was only this checklist

00:17:41.001 --> 00:17:43.855
that is external
memory structure that

00:17:43.856 --> 00:17:46.980
allows recurrent neural networks

00:17:46.981 --> 00:17:48.720
to better manage what's

00:17:48.721 --> 00:17:50.525
happening in
the long-term context.

00:17:50.526 --> 00:17:51.770
And that's going to be

00:17:51.771 --> 00:17:54.930
a repeating message in
the first half of this talk.

00:17:54.931 --> 00:17:59.625
So, works really well when
the input is a familiar and

00:17:59.626 --> 00:18:02.125
apparently skillet chicken rises

00:18:02.126 --> 00:18:04.730
quite common recipe
in the dataset.

00:18:04.731 --> 00:18:07.989
So, and this is a human recipe,

00:18:07.990 --> 00:18:10.400
it's probably
a little bit better.

00:18:10.401 --> 00:18:13.990
So, let's look at
the failure case.

00:18:13.991 --> 00:18:15.885
Chocolate covered
the potato chips.

00:18:15.886 --> 00:18:19.175
Now, never heard of this before.

00:18:19.176 --> 00:18:22.685
And baseline, preheat oven,

00:18:22.686 --> 00:18:25.050
probably a good idea
but then grease,

00:18:25.051 --> 00:18:29.170
flour a pan, start
baking it right away.

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:33.135
So, because input is

00:18:33.136 --> 00:18:35.320
on formula it doesn't know
what to do with this.

00:18:35.321 --> 00:18:38.699
Just ignore it. In
the case of checklist,

00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:40.050
it's paying more attention.

00:18:40.051 --> 00:18:41.460
It also does weird thing like

00:18:41.461 --> 00:18:43.260
suddenly bake
something right away.

00:18:43.261 --> 00:18:45.010
But then you realize, "Oh,

00:18:45.011 --> 00:18:46.620
I haven't done anything
with chocolate,

00:18:46.621 --> 00:18:48.005
so let's melt it."

00:18:48.006 --> 00:18:50.480
Then it says silly things like,

00:18:50.481 --> 00:18:52.835
"Add the potato mixture
to the potato mixture."

00:18:52.836 --> 00:18:55.075
Which is grammatically correct,

00:18:55.076 --> 00:18:57.120
it's just a little bit silly.

00:18:57.121 --> 00:19:00.570
Eventually, it's going to
fry that in the hot oil

00:19:00.571 --> 00:19:02.440
and I don't think
it's a good idea to

00:19:02.441 --> 00:19:05.550
fry something covered
in melted chocolate.

00:19:05.551 --> 00:19:09.755
So, that says something
about AI safety.

00:19:09.756 --> 00:19:13.100
I'm not joking here.

00:19:13.101 --> 00:19:17.430
So, the fundamental problem
with neural networks today

00:19:17.431 --> 00:19:19.520
despite all this
amazing results is that

00:19:19.521 --> 00:19:21.950
they struggle for
unfamiliar input,

00:19:21.951 --> 00:19:24.140
and as a result,

00:19:24.141 --> 00:19:27.365
we cannot really
really trust them.

00:19:27.366 --> 00:19:29.390
So, human recipe is much better.

00:19:29.391 --> 00:19:31.875
I'm going to come back
to this point in a bit.

00:19:31.876 --> 00:19:34.025
But let me very briefly mention

00:19:34.026 --> 00:19:37.190
how the same model was able

00:19:37.191 --> 00:19:40.945
to achieve better performance on

00:19:40.946 --> 00:19:45.555
a particular dialogue response
dataset as well.

00:19:45.556 --> 00:19:50.160
So, moving on to the teaser of

00:19:50.161 --> 00:19:52.300
the neural process networks which

00:19:52.301 --> 00:19:54.930
I'm going to give you
a motivation for.

00:19:54.931 --> 00:19:58.420
So, this is another example.

00:19:58.490 --> 00:20:02.245
''Deep-fried cauliflower''
that was the title.

00:20:02.246 --> 00:20:04.280
And then neural checklist

00:20:04.281 --> 00:20:06.100
the model generate the following.

00:20:06.101 --> 00:20:08.150
Wash and dry the cauliflower.

00:20:08.151 --> 00:20:09.775
Heat the oil in the skillet,

00:20:09.776 --> 00:20:12.480
and fry the source until
they're golden brown.

00:20:12.481 --> 00:20:14.170
Drain on paper towels at

00:20:14.171 --> 00:20:15.940
the source to the source,
and mix well.

00:20:15.941 --> 00:20:18.980
Serve hot or cold.
What's wrong with this?

00:20:20.780 --> 00:20:23.880
Yeah. Cauliflower, it's a steal.

00:20:23.881 --> 00:20:28.780
It's a clean, but
hasn't been cooked yet.

00:20:31.700 --> 00:20:35.755
I guess cauliflower is
okay to eat raw but.

00:20:35.756 --> 00:20:38.730
So, when I gave this talk about

00:20:38.731 --> 00:20:41.660
Neural Checklist Model
at Harvard University,

00:20:41.661 --> 00:20:43.930
a student asked me
after the talk,

00:20:43.931 --> 00:20:46.155
are RNN's a mouth
without a brain?

00:20:46.156 --> 00:20:48.485
I wasn't sure how
to respond to that,

00:20:48.486 --> 00:20:50.880
I guess I agreed with her.

00:20:50.881 --> 00:20:54.290
So, we need
common sense knowledge

00:20:54.291 --> 00:20:57.865
to reason about this sort
of unfamiliar situations.

00:20:57.866 --> 00:21:00.685
Humans can still do
the right thing whereas,

00:21:00.686 --> 00:21:03.430
we don't know what's
motions we'll do,

00:21:03.431 --> 00:21:04.890
neural networks will do.

00:21:04.891 --> 00:21:10.680
So, that's the motivation
like can we think

00:21:10.681 --> 00:21:14.305
about new architecture
that could read

00:21:14.306 --> 00:21:16.410
between the lines
and reason about

00:21:16.411 --> 00:21:18.650
the unspoken, but
obvious effects.

00:21:18.651 --> 00:21:21.100
For example, Fry tofu in the pan.

00:21:21.101 --> 00:21:23.500
Just common sense
wise, we know that,

00:21:23.501 --> 00:21:25.240
the location of tofu must have

00:21:25.241 --> 00:21:27.340
been the pan and
temperature of tofu must to

00:21:27.341 --> 00:21:28.860
become hot even though

00:21:28.861 --> 00:21:31.715
the sentence doesn't
say any of this.

00:21:31.716 --> 00:21:36.410
So, in a way it is similar to
how we might be able to do

00:21:36.411 --> 00:21:38.380
mental simulation of about a lot

00:21:38.381 --> 00:21:40.160
of things when we understand

00:21:40.161 --> 00:21:41.680
the stories and understand

00:21:41.681 --> 00:21:45.090
the others when making
conversation with them.

00:21:45.091 --> 00:21:46.320
In fact, there have been

00:21:46.321 --> 00:21:51.315
some literature in cognitive
science or psychology that,

00:21:51.316 --> 00:21:53.510
that's what humans
seem to be doing.

00:21:53.511 --> 00:21:56.215
Exactly how, is always debatable,

00:21:56.216 --> 00:21:59.290
but we seem to be doing
something like this.

00:21:59.291 --> 00:22:04.050
Can we then challenged our self
by asking this question,

00:22:04.051 --> 00:22:05.130
instead of this question.

00:22:05.131 --> 00:22:09.725
So, a lot of NLP research
has been based on labeling.

00:22:09.726 --> 00:22:11.950
Let's label every word
in a sentence

00:22:11.951 --> 00:22:14.555
with a syntactic and
semantic categories.

00:22:14.556 --> 00:22:17.405
That has been really very useful,

00:22:17.406 --> 00:22:19.720
but it focuses by-and-large

00:22:19.721 --> 00:22:22.060
on what is said in a sentence.

00:22:22.061 --> 00:22:23.740
Can we also think about,

00:22:23.741 --> 00:22:25.210
in addition to that,

00:22:25.211 --> 00:22:27.590
simulating the
causal effect that's

00:22:27.591 --> 00:22:29.765
obvious but not spoken,

00:22:29.766 --> 00:22:33.340
and abstracting away from
the surface strings.

00:22:33.341 --> 00:22:34.720
In fact, there have been

00:22:34.721 --> 00:22:37.535
some recent efforts
of that flavor,

00:22:37.536 --> 00:22:39.730
but of course when
people write in

00:22:39.731 --> 00:22:41.920
papers at the beginning,

00:22:41.921 --> 00:22:44.555
no paper will solve
anything completely.

00:22:44.556 --> 00:22:47.710
So, we have another one

00:22:47.711 --> 00:22:49.950
coming up, Neural
Processing Network.

00:22:49.951 --> 00:22:53.110
You can hear more about
this in a bit today,

00:22:53.111 --> 00:22:55.140
but I will leave
it there and then

00:22:55.141 --> 00:22:57.145
talk about the next component,

00:22:57.146 --> 00:23:00.080
Dynamic Entities in
Neural Language Models.

00:23:00.081 --> 00:23:04.540
So, lets work with
other colleagues at Youdot.

00:23:04.541 --> 00:23:07.755
You know she was
here, she left today.

00:23:07.756 --> 00:23:12.595
So, the problem with
neural networks,

00:23:12.596 --> 00:23:15.030
this is another sample
that I found it from

00:23:15.031 --> 00:23:19.760
a paper that introduced
LAMBADA dataset, I think.

00:23:19.761 --> 00:23:21.600
Human says, "What's your job"?

00:23:21.601 --> 00:23:22.840
Machine says, "I'm a lawyer".

00:23:22.841 --> 00:23:24.080
Human says, "What do you do"?

00:23:24.081 --> 00:23:27.150
Now, machine says,
"I'm a doctor too".

00:23:27.650 --> 00:23:30.740
Somewhat understandable,
if you think

00:23:30.741 --> 00:23:32.920
about how I embedding here,

00:23:32.921 --> 00:23:34.165
and I embedding here.

00:23:34.166 --> 00:23:36.540
When it starts with
a word embedding,

00:23:36.541 --> 00:23:38.330
it's studying like
it's the same word,

00:23:38.331 --> 00:23:39.550
it's the same word.

00:23:39.551 --> 00:23:43.080
So, Jane and Jane Eyre,

00:23:43.081 --> 00:23:45.650
maybe is same as
Jane in Mentalist,

00:23:45.651 --> 00:23:49.620
in terms of how they start
with neural representation.

00:23:49.621 --> 00:23:53.975
Then, when I say
she to mean Jane,

00:23:53.976 --> 00:23:57.310
now suddenly, they kind of
look like different words.

00:23:57.311 --> 00:23:59.180
In addition to that,

00:23:59.181 --> 00:24:01.610
there's this philosophical
question of,

00:24:01.611 --> 00:24:03.695
"Is it possible to encode

00:24:03.696 --> 00:24:07.790
very large context into one
vector"? Ray said, "No.

00:24:07.791 --> 00:24:11.810
It's not possible". I don't know,

00:24:11.811 --> 00:24:14.100
maybe some of you in
the audience were

00:24:14.101 --> 00:24:16.380
very young babies have
not known about this,

00:24:16.381 --> 00:24:17.835
but he said that.

00:24:17.836 --> 00:24:20.120
I'm going to just
repeat what he said

00:24:20.121 --> 00:24:22.740
by adding that nor into
a sequence of vectors,

00:24:22.741 --> 00:24:23.960
it's just not enough.

00:24:23.961 --> 00:24:27.650
So, recurrent language model,

00:24:27.651 --> 00:24:29.855
has a sequence of
vectors basically,

00:24:29.856 --> 00:24:34.020
and our model is to add

00:24:34.021 --> 00:24:36.600
more external memory
that can have

00:24:36.601 --> 00:24:40.445
a better long-term
representation, as follows.

00:24:40.446 --> 00:24:43.935
Let's just say, these
were the sum entity,

00:24:43.936 --> 00:24:47.965
and then I'm going to create
this separate memory chain,

00:24:47.966 --> 00:24:51.510
which is also recurrent
for some time.

00:24:51.511 --> 00:24:54.290
Whenever I see a
new entity mention,

00:24:54.291 --> 00:24:56.165
I'm going to create a new chain,

00:24:56.166 --> 00:24:58.390
and they are going to be

00:24:58.391 --> 00:25:00.620
just copying
themselves over time,

00:25:00.621 --> 00:25:03.480
until there's a co-reference to

00:25:03.481 --> 00:25:07.130
the same blue entity at
which point I'm going to

00:25:07.131 --> 00:25:10.520
now combine the
context coming from

00:25:10.521 --> 00:25:12.190
the recurrent neural network

00:25:12.191 --> 00:25:14.750
with whatever I was storing here,

00:25:14.751 --> 00:25:17.685
as a separate memory chain
and, so forth.

00:25:17.686 --> 00:25:21.475
So, that's the gist
of this model.

00:25:21.476 --> 00:25:25.985
Let's have a separate chain
in which we store

00:25:25.986 --> 00:25:28.540
a little bit more precise
information about

00:25:28.541 --> 00:25:30.200
different entity mentions

00:25:30.201 --> 00:25:32.405
corresponding to the same entity.

00:25:32.406 --> 00:25:36.280
In doing so, we are now

00:25:36.281 --> 00:25:38.900
having recurrent entity model

00:25:38.901 --> 00:25:41.805
in addition to the recurrent
language model.

00:25:41.806 --> 00:25:45.310
Whenever we need to compute

00:25:45.311 --> 00:25:50.360
new entity embedding
in this chain,

00:25:50.361 --> 00:25:51.920
we are going to interpolate that

00:25:51.921 --> 00:25:54.380
between whatever was coming from

00:25:54.381 --> 00:25:56.670
the Neural Network Language Model

00:25:56.671 --> 00:25:59.835
part and then whatever
was stored here.

00:25:59.836 --> 00:26:02.335
That interpolation
is based on gating,

00:26:02.336 --> 00:26:06.335
so, that's a fairly
standard thing to do.

00:26:06.336 --> 00:26:10.805
Gating can be parameterized
in many different ways.

00:26:10.806 --> 00:26:13.665
This is a particular way
of parameterizing it,

00:26:13.666 --> 00:26:16.080
but doesn't have to be this way.

00:26:16.081 --> 00:26:18.420
So, once we have that,

00:26:18.421 --> 00:26:19.950
then, what can we do with this?

00:26:19.951 --> 00:26:24.180
Well, usually when
you sample words

00:26:24.181 --> 00:26:26.360
out equal in sequence

00:26:26.361 --> 00:26:29.165
in the language model
part like this,

00:26:29.166 --> 00:26:32.235
and when you do that instead of

00:26:32.236 --> 00:26:35.155
conditioning on just hidden state

00:26:35.156 --> 00:26:37.100
of the recurrent
neural network part,

00:26:37.101 --> 00:26:40.240
now we can condition also on

00:26:40.241 --> 00:26:44.160
whatever is relevant entity
for that part.

00:26:44.161 --> 00:26:48.075
By doing so, we just to have
more precise conditioning on

00:26:48.076 --> 00:26:50.000
the context to that is more

00:26:50.001 --> 00:26:52.050
relevant than how we

00:26:52.051 --> 00:26:53.500
do that will be
impossible otherwise.

00:26:53.501 --> 00:26:57.750
So, in some sense,

00:26:57.751 --> 00:26:59.800
feed forward on neural network
with two layers,

00:26:59.801 --> 00:27:01.810
you should be able to
approximate anything in theory,

00:27:01.811 --> 00:27:04.405
but nobody can train it
to do anything useful.

00:27:04.406 --> 00:27:06.990
That's why in the computer
vision community,

00:27:06.991 --> 00:27:10.900
we will be seeing a lot of
our new architectures like,

00:27:10.901 --> 00:27:14.000
Radio.net, DenseNet,
CondenseNet and, so forth.

00:27:14.001 --> 00:27:15.740
So similarly, I think we need to

00:27:15.741 --> 00:27:17.660
do more research like this.

00:27:17.661 --> 00:27:20.705
This may not be the best
architecture yet,

00:27:20.706 --> 00:27:23.720
but the kind of
architecture that allows

00:27:23.721 --> 00:27:27.200
us to carry on long-term
contexts more precisely.

00:27:27.201 --> 00:27:29.845
So, that's a sort
of the intuition.

00:27:29.846 --> 00:27:33.200
I'm going to give you
just a very brief intuition

00:27:33.201 --> 00:27:36.670
about what we do for
training and testing.

00:27:36.671 --> 00:27:42.140
We went with this idea of
a generative model, such that,

00:27:42.141 --> 00:27:44.140
for each word there are a bunch

00:27:44.141 --> 00:27:48.809
of bookkeeping variables
that we create,

00:27:48.810 --> 00:27:52.260
such as a boolean
variable that ask

00:27:52.261 --> 00:27:56.175
whether part of an entity or not.

00:27:56.176 --> 00:27:59.975
If so, which entity do I belong

00:27:59.976 --> 00:28:04.440
to and then what's the length,

00:28:04.441 --> 00:28:06.070
and there's this that

00:28:06.071 --> 00:28:08.525
decrease over time
until it gets to zero.

00:28:08.526 --> 00:28:09.895
It's one way to do that,

00:28:09.896 --> 00:28:11.760
there may be different
ways of doing it.

00:28:11.761 --> 00:28:16.600
Finally, coreference relations.

00:28:16.601 --> 00:28:19.870
This one just shows
how these variables

00:28:19.871 --> 00:28:23.870
might refer to
different information.

00:28:23.871 --> 00:28:27.915
So, in this world,

00:28:27.916 --> 00:28:29.490
we'll just assume that we have

00:28:29.491 --> 00:28:31.965
this perfect coreference chain

00:28:31.966 --> 00:28:34.505
that was given from the sky.

00:28:34.506 --> 00:28:37.830
That's probably unrealistic
assumption to make,

00:28:37.831 --> 00:28:40.600
but as a first step
that's what we did.

00:28:40.601 --> 00:28:43.010
Then, during testing of course,

00:28:43.011 --> 00:28:45.080
we seemed a little bit less

00:28:45.081 --> 00:28:48.020
cautious to assume
that for testing so,

00:28:48.021 --> 00:28:51.910
we just sample and
marginalize them out,

00:28:51.911 --> 00:28:54.630
using importance of sampling.

00:28:55.370 --> 00:28:58.880
By doing this then,
what can we do?

00:28:58.881 --> 00:29:01.580
We can then think about
different used cases.

00:29:01.581 --> 00:29:04.530
So, we first of all looked

00:29:04.531 --> 00:29:09.780
at just using these as
language model that may be

00:29:09.781 --> 00:29:14.455
able to marginalize
out different types of

00:29:14.456 --> 00:29:17.810
coreference decisions and
then in that case it can

00:29:17.811 --> 00:29:22.150
lower the perplexity on
the corner test dataset.

00:29:22.151 --> 00:29:24.430
Then in the second case,

00:29:24.431 --> 00:29:27.365
we also use the days in order to

00:29:27.366 --> 00:29:30.985
improve a certain coreference

00:29:30.986 --> 00:29:32.930
resolution system that we could

00:29:32.931 --> 00:29:35.045
piggyback on by adding

00:29:35.046 --> 00:29:39.335
our model scores as

00:29:39.336 --> 00:29:41.480
additional scores to
help with the ranking.

00:29:41.481 --> 00:29:44.560
So, we plot our system to ranking

00:29:44.561 --> 00:29:48.180
based system and we were able
to improve the performance.

00:29:48.181 --> 00:29:50.100
It's not state of the art because

00:29:50.101 --> 00:29:52.010
people keep improving
on this quite a bit and

00:29:52.011 --> 00:29:54.055
our colleague at Youdot's look

00:29:54.056 --> 00:29:56.545
has so much stronger results now.

00:29:56.546 --> 00:30:00.250
Then finally, there was
a very interesting new data

00:30:00.251 --> 00:30:04.500
set that was a little bit
common sense inspired,

00:30:04.501 --> 00:30:06.090
and it was about

00:30:06.091 --> 00:30:08.700
predicting what entity
may get mentioned next.

00:30:08.701 --> 00:30:10.350
Then, for that task goal,

00:30:10.351 --> 00:30:12.385
so we were able to perform

00:30:12.386 --> 00:30:14.760
much better than
other strong baselines.

00:30:14.761 --> 00:30:18.270
So, that's another story

00:30:18.271 --> 00:30:22.860
that repeats this theme
of or spirit

00:30:22.861 --> 00:30:24.510
of having new neural

00:30:24.511 --> 00:30:26.600
network architecture
that might better

00:30:26.601 --> 00:30:29.040
represent the latent process that

00:30:29.041 --> 00:30:31.660
goes in the way text is written,

00:30:31.661 --> 00:30:33.750
where text is understood.

00:30:33.751 --> 00:30:35.110
Then, now I'm going to

00:30:35.111 --> 00:30:36.670
switch the gear
quite a bit and talk

00:30:36.671 --> 00:30:40.530
about this VerbPhysics
which is more on this side.

00:30:41.300 --> 00:30:44.175
So, this work is

00:30:44.176 --> 00:30:47.735
motivated by this hope
that in the future,

00:30:47.736 --> 00:30:50.940
we may have a home robot
that should understand

00:30:50.941 --> 00:30:55.045
a lot about different
household items.

00:30:55.046 --> 00:30:58.500
So that it can
interact with them.

00:30:58.501 --> 00:31:01.180
You should have understanding
about relative size,

00:31:01.181 --> 00:31:03.925
way to reach the tear
strength and so forth.

00:31:03.926 --> 00:31:06.320
So, it's good to be

00:31:06.321 --> 00:31:09.140
aware that usually
people are larger than

00:31:09.141 --> 00:31:15.000
a chair and if I were to
look this up on Google,

00:31:15.001 --> 00:31:16.710
nobody says this, but

00:31:16.711 --> 00:31:19.170
now I said this so
you can search this,

00:31:19.171 --> 00:31:23.070
but when I search today
for the first time,

00:31:23.071 --> 00:31:25.720
I couldn't search
that or any of this,

00:31:25.721 --> 00:31:28.055
because nobody says any of this.

00:31:28.056 --> 00:31:30.940
It's trivially true, so this is

00:31:30.941 --> 00:31:32.690
a known factor as
a reporting bias

00:31:32.691 --> 00:31:33.850
that people don't state

00:31:33.851 --> 00:31:37.020
the obvious and then
when finally people say

00:31:37.021 --> 00:31:40.250
something it's exceptional case.

00:31:40.251 --> 00:31:42.135
So, we must not conclude that

00:31:42.136 --> 00:31:45.175
the horses are similar
in size to dogs.

00:31:45.176 --> 00:31:47.220
So then what do we do,

00:31:47.221 --> 00:31:51.025
I thought that the answer
must be in computer vision.

00:31:51.026 --> 00:31:52.575
Let's look at images,

00:31:52.576 --> 00:31:54.930
so we worked on this

00:31:54.931 --> 00:31:57.555
and one of the papers
looked at lots of

00:31:57.556 --> 00:32:02.990
images and estimated
relative size differences,

00:32:02.991 --> 00:32:04.840
adjusted where the depth of

00:32:04.841 --> 00:32:08.405
the differences and then
we were able to learn

00:32:08.406 --> 00:32:11.020
some knowledge like dogs
are usually bigger than

00:32:11.021 --> 00:32:14.015
cats but the takeaway

00:32:14.016 --> 00:32:16.560
from this work was that I thought

00:32:16.561 --> 00:32:20.385
NLPs are very hard but computer
vision is also hard.

00:32:20.386 --> 00:32:21.990
In addition to that,

00:32:21.991 --> 00:32:23.170
computer vision just takes

00:32:23.171 --> 00:32:26.395
more computation because
images are bigger to process.

00:32:26.396 --> 00:32:29.580
So, after processing
lots of images,

00:32:29.581 --> 00:32:33.670
I only got like hundreds
of knowledge nuggets which

00:32:33.671 --> 00:32:38.840
seemed a kind of small and
then in addition to that,

00:32:38.841 --> 00:32:41.065
size, we can measure,

00:32:41.066 --> 00:32:43.420
but what about relative weight or

00:32:43.421 --> 00:32:46.360
strength differences
or speed differences?

00:32:46.361 --> 00:32:49.480
These are visual to human eyes.

00:32:49.481 --> 00:32:53.040
Not really for computer
vision yet, to some degree,

00:32:53.041 --> 00:32:57.850
yes but nothing nor reliable way
that I could depend on.

00:32:57.851 --> 00:33:02.400
So, our revised plan
then is that, well,

00:33:02.401 --> 00:33:04.035
one could just wait

00:33:04.036 --> 00:33:05.890
but since we don't have
anything better to

00:33:05.891 --> 00:33:09.915
do let's go back to language
but with a different plan.

00:33:09.916 --> 00:33:13.750
So, key insight is this,

00:33:13.751 --> 00:33:16.570
even though nobody
says any of these,

00:33:16.571 --> 00:33:20.700
people do say that they
throw a pen or stone,

00:33:20.701 --> 00:33:21.780
chair, and so forth.

00:33:21.781 --> 00:33:25.245
So, all of these
are possible when

00:33:25.246 --> 00:33:29.819
the agent is typically
bigger than the object,

00:33:29.820 --> 00:33:32.745
heavier than
the object as a result

00:33:32.746 --> 00:33:37.430
the object is moving faster
than the agent temporarily.

00:33:37.490 --> 00:33:40.560
So, the representation which we

00:33:40.561 --> 00:33:43.335
named as VerbPhysics
goes as follows,

00:33:43.336 --> 00:33:47.990
for it's very similar
to frame semantics,

00:33:47.991 --> 00:33:50.500
but now it talks a little bit

00:33:50.501 --> 00:33:53.250
more about pragmatic
meanings of language.

00:33:53.251 --> 00:33:58.500
So, for any pair of potential
arguments of a predicate,

00:33:58.501 --> 00:34:01.410
we can think about
what may be implied,

00:34:01.411 --> 00:34:03.080
so we walked into the house,

00:34:03.081 --> 00:34:06.140
may imply that, "I am
probably smaller than

00:34:06.141 --> 00:34:08.130
my house and lighter than

00:34:08.131 --> 00:34:10.970
my house and move
faster than my house,

00:34:10.971 --> 00:34:14.755
and if I squash
a bug with my boots

00:34:14.756 --> 00:34:24.420
probably the bug is

00:34:24.421 --> 00:34:25.710
smaller than my boots

00:34:25.711 --> 00:34:27.705
and lighter than
my boot and so forth."

00:34:27.706 --> 00:34:32.865
So, they are sort of a
long list of obviously,

00:34:32.866 --> 00:34:36.300
likely truth about the world

00:34:36.301 --> 00:34:39.330
that comes to our
mind and perhaps,

00:34:39.331 --> 00:34:42.030
we can learn to
detect that as well.

00:34:42.031 --> 00:34:45.759
So, in terms of model,

00:34:46.970 --> 00:34:50.220
we decided that perhaps if we

00:34:50.221 --> 00:34:53.235
can solve two related problems

00:34:53.236 --> 00:34:54.870
simultaneously in

00:34:54.871 --> 00:34:58.920
particular fiscal properties
implied by verbs,

00:34:58.921 --> 00:35:02.600
so what kind of
actions to imply about

00:35:02.601 --> 00:35:06.570
different arguments
and then also in

00:35:06.571 --> 00:35:10.270
general different relative
knowledge in between objects.

00:35:10.271 --> 00:35:13.445
Across five different
attributes in this work,

00:35:13.446 --> 00:35:15.270
although one can extended

00:35:15.271 --> 00:35:18.305
it to include
other attributes as well,

00:35:18.306 --> 00:35:22.025
and if you've been waiting
for deep learning to pass,

00:35:22.026 --> 00:35:25.455
so in this work we didn't
use deep learning per se,

00:35:25.456 --> 00:35:28.380
although we did make use
of a neural embeddings.

00:35:28.381 --> 00:35:32.405
But otherwise, it's
a factor graph with lots of

00:35:32.406 --> 00:35:37.110
variables that have
a probabilistic interpretation.

00:35:37.111 --> 00:35:41.080
So, it goes something like that.

00:35:41.081 --> 00:35:43.660
I'm going to give you
a very sketched idea

00:35:43.661 --> 00:35:44.805
of what we're doing.

00:35:44.806 --> 00:35:46.950
So, we're going to
throw in a bunch

00:35:46.951 --> 00:35:49.590
of random variables
that might mean

00:35:49.591 --> 00:35:52.600
that for each of them
it might mean something

00:35:52.601 --> 00:35:56.295
like p is either
bigger or smaller,

00:35:56.296 --> 00:36:00.105
comparable to q in terms of size.

00:36:00.106 --> 00:36:01.530
So, they can have

00:36:01.531 --> 00:36:04.300
these three different
value assignment

00:36:04.301 --> 00:36:06.270
and then on the other side of

00:36:06.271 --> 00:36:08.895
the graph we can also
throw in a bunch of

00:36:08.896 --> 00:36:12.550
random variables that
encodes action implications,

00:36:12.551 --> 00:36:14.720
again the value can
be either bigger or

00:36:14.721 --> 00:36:17.020
smaller or same and then they

00:36:17.021 --> 00:36:20.270
can be about a particular
predicate like

00:36:20.271 --> 00:36:24.520
throw with respect to a
particular attribute like size.

00:36:24.521 --> 00:36:30.360
In fact, this predictive
variable is a collection of

00:36:30.361 --> 00:36:33.490
random variables
internally due to the fact

00:36:33.491 --> 00:36:36.830
that using the same
verb like throw,

00:36:36.831 --> 00:36:41.590
we can use in
many different frames,

00:36:41.591 --> 00:36:45.545
so we just use
a different random variables

00:36:45.546 --> 00:36:47.560
for different instance initiation

00:36:47.561 --> 00:36:50.085
of any particular predicate.

00:36:50.086 --> 00:36:52.440
But for now, we might
just assume that they're

00:36:52.441 --> 00:36:54.575
sort of like behaving similarly.

00:36:54.576 --> 00:36:56.850
Now, at this point though,

00:36:56.851 --> 00:36:58.980
there's no evidence about

00:36:58.981 --> 00:37:01.465
any of these random
variables truth assignment

00:37:01.466 --> 00:37:04.650
because nobody says
my house is bigger than me,

00:37:04.651 --> 00:37:06.740
and nobody says when
I throw something I

00:37:06.741 --> 00:37:08.860
am usually bigger
than the object,

00:37:08.861 --> 00:37:10.210
no one says any of this.

00:37:10.211 --> 00:37:14.425
So, the only thing that we
do observe from language is

00:37:14.426 --> 00:37:17.260
how people use
different arguments

00:37:17.261 --> 00:37:19.475
together with that
different actions,

00:37:19.476 --> 00:37:21.240
that for example I throw

00:37:21.241 --> 00:37:22.795
my bag as I walked into my house.

00:37:22.796 --> 00:37:24.600
So, people do say this and that's

00:37:24.601 --> 00:37:27.195
the relational evidence is

00:37:27.196 --> 00:37:29.425
the only evidence
that we do have.

00:37:29.426 --> 00:37:34.170
So, translating
this intuition into

00:37:34.171 --> 00:37:39.180
the graph model is that
we are throwing in

00:37:39.181 --> 00:37:42.135
potential function
that quantifies

00:37:42.136 --> 00:37:44.760
the selection of
preference between a verb

00:37:44.761 --> 00:37:47.440
and its arguments
and then similarly

00:37:47.441 --> 00:37:50.820
a lot of potential function,

00:37:50.821 --> 00:37:53.285
see for object-object similarity

00:37:53.286 --> 00:37:55.190
as well as verb-verb similarity,

00:37:55.191 --> 00:37:57.160
as well as a
frame-frame similarity.

00:37:57.161 --> 00:37:59.610
Then finally, we can not

00:37:59.611 --> 00:38:02.670
suddenly decode knowledge
out of nothing,

00:38:02.671 --> 00:38:06.330
so there's a little bit of
received knowledge given as

00:38:06.331 --> 00:38:08.470
unary potential function so

00:38:08.471 --> 00:38:12.415
that we can reason about
the entire network.

00:38:12.416 --> 00:38:14.140
So far, we have been only talking

00:38:14.141 --> 00:38:15.550
about size but we can keep

00:38:15.551 --> 00:38:18.400
doing this until
the network gets very,

00:38:18.401 --> 00:38:20.040
very messy by adding

00:38:20.041 --> 00:38:22.735
this weight part and
other parts as well.

00:38:22.736 --> 00:38:27.470
So, there's this
well-known inference,

00:38:27.471 --> 00:38:30.155
algorithm known as Loopy
Belief Propagation,

00:38:30.156 --> 00:38:32.880
that's what we did for making

00:38:32.881 --> 00:38:35.755
the inference and the conclusion.

00:38:35.756 --> 00:38:37.850
Very briefly is that

00:38:37.851 --> 00:38:40.745
the random assignment
would be one third,

00:38:40.746 --> 00:38:46.740
accuracy, majority baseline
will be about to 44 to 50.

00:38:46.741 --> 00:38:52.620
Whereas, if we just use neural
embeddings to reason about

00:38:52.621 --> 00:38:55.950
different knowledge without
the entire graph but

00:38:55.951 --> 00:38:59.605
just one note independently
from all the other,

00:38:59.606 --> 00:39:02.250
then that's how much we do,

00:39:02.251 --> 00:39:04.910
and then having
this graph inference

00:39:04.911 --> 00:39:08.715
can help improving
the performance further.

00:39:08.716 --> 00:39:11.930
On one side we are looking
at frame predictions,

00:39:11.931 --> 00:39:13.440
on the other side
we are looking at

00:39:13.441 --> 00:39:15.690
object-object knowledge.

00:39:15.691 --> 00:39:18.675
So to summarize,

00:39:18.676 --> 00:39:21.490
we have explored to whether

00:39:21.491 --> 00:39:23.900
it's possible to reverse
engineer neither

00:39:23.901 --> 00:39:26.030
physics that knowledge
from language even

00:39:26.031 --> 00:39:29.200
though people don't speak
the obvious effects.

00:39:29.201 --> 00:39:35.370
But perhaps we can design
a model or inference algorithm

00:39:35.371 --> 00:39:38.110
that could still be
able to reason about

00:39:38.111 --> 00:39:42.145
unspoken but what we all
assume with each other,

00:39:42.146 --> 00:39:43.970
which systematically

00:39:43.971 --> 00:39:46.120
influences the way
people use language,

00:39:46.121 --> 00:39:47.340
which then give us some clue

00:39:47.341 --> 00:39:49.520
about what may be
true about the world.

00:39:49.521 --> 00:39:52.345
We can do this
without having robots

00:39:52.346 --> 00:39:55.830
that can have an embodiment
and interact with the world.

00:39:55.831 --> 00:39:58.065
All right, so now I'm going to

00:39:58.066 --> 00:40:00.230
conclude with some remarks about

00:40:00.231 --> 00:40:06.015
what future research directions
we might pursue further.

00:40:06.016 --> 00:40:09.970
So as far as neural
network architecture,

00:40:09.971 --> 00:40:12.060
there have been
some debates about whether

00:40:12.061 --> 00:40:14.240
there should be
innate architecture or not.

00:40:14.241 --> 00:40:17.755
I'm obviously on the side of
having innate architecture.

00:40:17.756 --> 00:40:19.910
I think in the NLP community

00:40:19.911 --> 00:40:21.680
there have been more emphasis,

00:40:21.681 --> 00:40:23.450
relatively speaking
more emphasis on

00:40:23.451 --> 00:40:27.150
the linguistic
sentential structure

00:40:27.151 --> 00:40:29.380
where they usually are

00:40:29.381 --> 00:40:31.500
some explicit structure and

00:40:31.501 --> 00:40:33.190
it's a very, very well-studied.

00:40:33.191 --> 00:40:35.130
Whereas, the moment we
start thinking about

00:40:35.131 --> 00:40:37.605
document level or
discourse level or

00:40:37.606 --> 00:40:39.510
just generally long-term context

00:40:39.511 --> 00:40:42.910
or latent processes of
how the world works,

00:40:42.911 --> 00:40:47.350
things have become much less
clear how to handle this.

00:40:47.351 --> 00:40:48.570
Nonetheless, I think this part

00:40:48.571 --> 00:40:50.070
is very important to proceed

00:40:50.071 --> 00:40:53.610
further and then as
various formalism,

00:40:53.611 --> 00:40:55.640
I think we should really start

00:40:55.641 --> 00:40:56.790
thinking more about like what

00:40:56.791 --> 00:40:57.920
to do with the common sense.

00:40:57.921 --> 00:41:00.470
I was told not to use
the word there for some time

00:41:00.471 --> 00:41:04.540
and I think it's because it was

00:41:04.541 --> 00:41:07.365
major failure before
but I realize

00:41:07.366 --> 00:41:11.160
at some point that it's
just nonsense to conclude

00:41:11.161 --> 00:41:14.400
that that direction is going
to be forever impossible

00:41:14.401 --> 00:41:16.310
when the past failures were

00:41:16.311 --> 00:41:18.445
based on we're computing power,

00:41:18.446 --> 00:41:21.490
not much data, no crowd sourcing

00:41:21.491 --> 00:41:24.435
and not much computational

00:41:24.436 --> 00:41:26.665
as a strong computational models,

00:41:26.666 --> 00:41:29.035
and also it was done by

00:41:29.036 --> 00:41:31.170
non-NLP people and I

00:41:31.171 --> 00:41:33.830
think with language we
can do a better job,

00:41:33.831 --> 00:41:35.705
or we should try

00:41:35.706 --> 00:41:39.070
before concluding that
it's not going to work.

00:41:39.071 --> 00:41:41.905
So, in terms of
physical common sense,

00:41:41.906 --> 00:41:46.030
the potential impact cases
could be more with

00:41:46.031 --> 00:41:49.690
zero-shot/few-shot reasoning
cases especially when

00:41:49.691 --> 00:41:53.845
there's language and vision
and robotics applications.

00:41:53.846 --> 00:41:56.890
But we can also do
similar things with

00:41:56.891 --> 00:41:58.190
the social common sense

00:41:58.191 --> 00:42:00.090
which I haven't
talked about today.

00:42:00.091 --> 00:42:02.410
However, we have been
working something along

00:42:02.411 --> 00:42:05.400
this line with
connotation frames and also

00:42:05.401 --> 00:42:07.725
we now have a new
ACL paper coming up

00:42:07.726 --> 00:42:10.660
which I'm going to give
you a teaser about.

00:42:10.661 --> 00:42:14.775
So, it's going to be about
based on the rock stories

00:42:14.776 --> 00:42:16.585
developed at Microsoft in

00:42:16.586 --> 00:42:19.120
Rochester where the story
goes like this,

00:42:19.121 --> 00:42:22.835
"The band instructor told
the band to start playing.

00:42:22.836 --> 00:42:26.420
He often stopped to the music
when players were off-tone.

00:42:26.421 --> 00:42:28.550
They grew tired and
started playing worse

00:42:28.551 --> 00:42:30.585
after awhile and so forth."

00:42:30.586 --> 00:42:35.190
So, when there are these sort
of people involved in

00:42:35.191 --> 00:42:38.310
our story we can
oftentimes reason

00:42:38.311 --> 00:42:41.535
about what people
might feel like here.

00:42:41.536 --> 00:42:44.925
The instructor was furious
and threw his chair.

00:42:44.926 --> 00:42:47.280
It doesn't say what
these guys feel

00:42:47.281 --> 00:42:50.560
now about their situation
but we can imagine

00:42:50.561 --> 00:42:53.760
that they must feel
fear or feel angry or

00:42:53.761 --> 00:42:57.120
sad even though it's
not mentioned here.

00:42:57.121 --> 00:43:00.635
So, we can sort of
have this annotation,

00:43:00.636 --> 00:43:04.640
very low-level annotation
about what people might feel,

00:43:04.641 --> 00:43:06.980
what people's motivation might be

00:43:06.981 --> 00:43:09.710
before and after
different actions and events.

00:43:09.711 --> 00:43:12.690
We have a bunch of
new annotations and

00:43:12.691 --> 00:43:16.725
baseline models reported
coming up soon.

00:43:16.726 --> 00:43:20.475
Then, related work also in ACL

00:43:20.476 --> 00:43:24.205
will be about
common sense inference.

00:43:24.206 --> 00:43:27.315
For example, if someone
coextensive giving dinner,

00:43:27.316 --> 00:43:32.115
probably that person's intent
is to impress their family.

00:43:32.116 --> 00:43:36.145
Afterwards, maybe
they will feel tired

00:43:36.146 --> 00:43:40.660
but still feel a sense of
belonging and finally,

00:43:40.661 --> 00:43:42.330
other people even though they are

00:43:42.331 --> 00:43:44.270
not mentioned in the sentence,

00:43:44.271 --> 00:43:47.550
they will probably feel
impressed or happy and so forth.

00:43:47.551 --> 00:43:51.290
So, we can reason about
what people might

00:43:51.291 --> 00:43:53.260
typically do before and

00:43:53.261 --> 00:43:55.120
after in terms of
their mental states,

00:43:55.121 --> 00:43:57.299
so it's also coming up at ACL

00:43:57.300 --> 00:44:00.150
with a bunch of new dataset.

00:44:00.151 --> 00:44:02.790
So finally, I'd like to say

00:44:02.791 --> 00:44:07.110
that this new modelling power,

00:44:07.111 --> 00:44:09.490
those creates
some new opportunities for

00:44:09.491 --> 00:44:12.030
doing more human-centric
applications.

00:44:12.031 --> 00:44:14.675
So that people don't fear just AI

00:44:14.676 --> 00:44:17.615
as a negative
potentially scary thing.

00:44:17.616 --> 00:44:23.095
I think we could try doing
more positive x in the space.

00:44:23.096 --> 00:44:26.310
Personally, I think we should
work more on interactive AI

00:44:26.311 --> 00:44:28.030
because that's what
the robotics people

00:44:28.031 --> 00:44:30.090
really want as well.

00:44:30.920 --> 00:44:33.330
Especially with
the neural network,

00:44:33.331 --> 00:44:34.635
it seems so that
we can do a lot of

00:44:34.636 --> 00:44:37.340
creative things in
texture generation too.

00:44:37.341 --> 00:44:39.460
So, I will just read in

00:44:39.461 --> 00:44:43.030
this sonnet that was
generated by a machine.

00:44:43.031 --> 00:44:46.345
So, Turing was the title
and it goes like this,

00:44:46.346 --> 00:44:49.090
"Creating some
electric slot machine,

00:44:49.091 --> 00:44:51.335
of hearing those
familiar voices say,

00:44:51.336 --> 00:44:53.560
'Awake behind
an empty picture mean,

00:44:53.561 --> 00:44:55.670
the windows open over Alan Kay.'

00:44:55.671 --> 00:44:57.970
Consider me of
regular expression,

00:44:57.971 --> 00:45:00.310
or maybe something
very quickly typed.

00:45:00.311 --> 00:45:02.445
And I forget about
on old obsession,

00:45:02.446 --> 00:45:04.835
a hundred thousand people
getting hyped.

00:45:04.836 --> 00:45:07.270
Become the biggest part
of my computer,

00:45:07.271 --> 00:45:09.370
and take another journey
down the road.

00:45:09.371 --> 00:45:11.860
Or set a balance on
a minor scooter,

00:45:11.861 --> 00:45:14.190
or even matter whose enigma code.

00:45:14.191 --> 00:45:16.320
And music takes control
of all the means,

00:45:16.321 --> 00:45:19.680
surrounded by a world
of a strange machines."

00:45:20.960 --> 00:45:24.155
Couldn't fool anybody yet.

00:45:24.156 --> 00:45:28.210
Thank you.

